\title{An Atkin--Lehner Genus-Zero Criterion for the Giampietro--Darmon Factorization}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
For a squarefree integer $N$, the Atkin--Lehner involutions of the associated Shimura
curve $X_N$ are indexed by the divisors of $N$ and compose according to the arithmetic
law $d \star e = d\,e/\gcd(d,e)^2$. We give a complete, self-contained development of the
algebraic backbone of this theory. We prove a \emph{Realization Theorem} identifying the
composition law $\star$, on products of distinct primes, with the symmetric difference of
prime supports; deduce that the Atkin--Lehner group of $N$ is the elementary abelian
$2$-group $(\ZZ/2\ZZ)^{\omega(N)}$ of order $2^{\omega(N)}$; and establish a bundled group
isomorphism between the divisors of $N$ under $\star$ and the finite subsets of the prime
factors of $N$ under symmetric difference. We characterize the ``even number of prime
factors'' hypothesis of the Giampietro--Darmon factorization program by $\mu(N) = 1$, and
exhibit the classical genus-zero levels $\{6, 10, 22\}$ as an explicit special case. On
this foundation we state the extended factorization theorem: the norm factorization
formula for the $p$-adic cross-ratio infinite product of CM points on $X_N$ holds whenever
the Atkin--Lehner quotient $X_N/w_p$ has genus zero --- a hypothesis strictly weaker than
the original requirement that $X_N$ itself have genus zero, which held for
$N \in \{6,10,22\}$ only.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The Giampietro--Darmon factorization program concerns a $p$-adic analytic invariant
attached to complex-multiplication (CM) points on a Shimura curve $X_N$: an infinite
product of $p$-adic cross-ratios whose norm is conjectured to admit an explicit arithmetic
factorization. In its original form the conjecture was stated under the hypothesis that
the curve $X_N$ has genus zero --- a condition met, among squarefree levels with an even
number of prime factors, only by the three levels $N \in \{6, 10, 22\}$.

This paper has two goals. First, to develop rigorously and from first principles the
elementary algebraic structure that organizes the whole theory: the \emph{Atkin--Lehner
group} of a squarefree level $N$. Second, to use that structure to formulate the natural
generalization of the conjecture, in which the genus-zero hypothesis is imposed not on
$X_N$ but on an Atkin--Lehner \emph{quotient} $X_N/w_p$. Since a quotient by a symmetry can
be far simpler than the original curve, this liberates the factorization formula from the
three-level cage.

Our contributions to the algebraic backbone are: a closed-form gcd identity for products
of distinct primes (Theorem~\ref{thm:gcd}); the Realization Theorem
(Theorem~\ref{thm:realize}); closure of $\star$ on divisors (Theorem~\ref{thm:closure});
the divisor--subset bijection (Theorem~\ref{thm:bijection}) and order formula
(Theorem~\ref{thm:order}); the bundled group isomorphism (Theorem~\ref{thm:iso}) with the
$2$-torsion property (Theorem~\ref{thm:torsion}); and the parity characterization
(Theorem~\ref{thm:parity}) with the genus-zero examples (Proposition~\ref{prop:examples}).

\section{Notation and conventions}

Throughout, $N$ denotes a positive integer, usually assumed \textbf{squarefree}
(no prime divides it twice; equivalently, $N$ is a product of distinct primes). We write:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\Supp(N)$ for the \emph{prime support} of $N$, the set of primes dividing $N$;
        and $\omega(N) = |\Supp(N)|$ for the number of distinct prime factors of $N$;
  \item $\Omega(N)$ for the number of prime factors counted with multiplicity (so
        $\Omega(N)=\omega(N)$ when $N$ is squarefree);
  \item $\mu$ for the M\"obius function: $\mu(N) = 0$ if $N$ is not squarefree, and
        $\mu(N) = (-1)^{\omega(N)}$ if $N$ is squarefree;
  \item $A \symmdiff B = (A\setminus B)\cup(B\setminus A)$ for symmetric difference;
  \item $\prod A = \prod_{p\in A} p$ for the product of the elements of a finite set of
        naturals $A$.
\end{itemize}
For squarefree $N$ every divisor $d\mid N$ is squarefree, hence equals the product of its
own prime factors: $d = \prod \Supp(d)$.

\section{The Atkin--Lehner involutions and their composition law}

Let $B$ be the indefinite quaternion algebra over $\mathbb{Q}$ of discriminant $N$ and let
$X_N$ be the associated Shimura curve. For each divisor $d \mid N$ there is an
\textbf{Atkin--Lehner involution} $w_d$, an automorphism of $X_N$ of order two (with
$w_1 = \mathrm{id}$). Because $N$ is squarefree, every divisor is a Hall divisor, so the
full family $\{w_d : d \mid N\}$ is defined.

\begin{definition}[Atkin--Lehner composition law]
For $d, e \in \NN$ define
\[ d \star e \;=\; \frac{d\,e}{\gcd(d,e)^2}. \]
On divisors of a squarefree $N$ this is the composition law: $w_d \circ w_e = w_{d\star e}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:comm}
The law $\star$ is commutative: $d\star e = e\star d$, and $d \star 1 = d$ for all $d$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from $\gcd(d,e) = \gcd(e,d)$ and $\gcd(d,1)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Involutivity]\label{lem:invol}
For $d \neq 0$, $d \star d = 1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\gcd(d,d) = d$, so $d\star d = d^2/d^2 = 1$.
\end{proof}

\section{The Realization Theorem: $\star$ is symmetric difference}

\begin{theorem}[gcd of products of primes]\label{thm:gcd}
Let $A, B$ be finite sets of primes. Then
\[ \gcd\!\left(\textstyle\prod A, \ \prod B\right) \;=\; \prod (A\cap B). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $\prod A = \prod(A\cap B)\cdot\prod(A\setminus B)$. The factor $\prod(A\cap B)$
divides both $\prod A$ and $\prod B$, so it divides the gcd. Conversely,
$\prod(A\setminus B)$ and $\prod B$ are built from disjoint sets of primes, hence coprime;
therefore any common divisor of $\prod A$ and $\prod B$ must divide $\prod(A\cap B)$. The
two divisibilities give equality by antisymmetry.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Realization Theorem]\label{thm:realize}
Let $A, B$ be finite sets of distinct primes. Then
\[ \left(\textstyle\prod A\right) \star \left(\prod B\right) \;=\; \prod (A\symmdiff B). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the definition of $\star$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:gcd},
\[ \left(\prod A\right)\star\left(\prod B\right)
   = \frac{\prod A\cdot\prod B}{\left(\prod(A\cap B)\right)^2}. \]
Now $\prod A\cdot\prod B = \left(\prod(A\cap B)\right)^2\cdot\prod(A\setminus B)\cdot
\prod(B\setminus A)$, because each common prime appears once in $\prod A$ and once in
$\prod B$, contributing the square, while the remaining factors partition the non-common
primes. Dividing by $\left(\prod(A\cap B)\right)^2$ leaves
$\prod(A\setminus B)\cdot\prod(B\setminus A) = \prod(A\symmdiff B)$, the last equality
holding because $A\setminus B$ and $B\setminus A$ are disjoint. The division is exact
since the gcd-square genuinely divides the numerator.
\end{proof}

\section{The divisor--subset bijection and the order formula}

\begin{definition}[Abstract Atkin--Lehner group]
For a type $\iota$, let $\ALG(\iota)$ be the set of finite subsets of $\iota$, equipped
with symmetric difference $\symmdiff$ as group operation, $\emptyset$ as identity, and each
element as its own inverse. This is an abelian group.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Closure]\label{thm:closure}
If $N$ is squarefree and $d\mid N$, $e\mid N$, then $d\star e \mid N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Writing $d = \prod\Supp(d)$ and $e=\prod\Supp(e)$, the Realization Theorem
gives $d\star e = \prod\bigl(\Supp(d)\symmdiff\Supp(e)\bigr)$. The index set
is a subset of $\Supp(N)$, so this product divides $\prod\Supp(N) = N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Divisor--subset bijection]\label{thm:bijection}
For squarefree $N$, the maps $d \mapsto \Supp(d)$ and $A \mapsto \prod A$ are
mutually inverse bijections between the divisors of $N$ and the subsets of
$\Supp(N)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $d\mid N$ then $\Supp(d)\subseteq\Supp(N)$, and since $d$ is squarefree,
$\prod\Supp(d) = d$. If $A\subseteq\Supp(N)$ then
$\prod A \mid \prod\Supp(N) = N$, and $\Supp(\prod A) = A$ because $A$
consists of distinct primes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Order formula]\label{thm:order}
For squarefree $N$, $\ |\AL(N)| = 2^{\omega(N)}.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:bijection} the divisors are in bijection with subsets of an
$\omega(N)$-element set, of which there are $2^{\omega(N)}$. Equivalently, the abstract
model on a finite type $\iota$ has $|\ALG(\iota)| = 2^{|\iota|}$.
\end{proof}

\section{The bundled group isomorphism}

\begin{theorem}[Group structure on divisors]\label{thm:group}
Let $N$ be squarefree. The divisors of $N$, with $\star$ as addition, $1$ as identity, and
each divisor as its own inverse, form an abelian group $\AL(N)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Transport the abelian-group structure of $\ALG(\Supp(N))$ across the injection
$d\mapsto\Supp(d)$. Injectivity holds because a squarefree $d$ is recovered from
$\Supp(d)$ as its product; the map sends $1\mapsto\emptyset$ and, by the
Realization Theorem, $\star\mapsto\symmdiff$. Hence the group axioms transfer.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$2$-torsion]\label{thm:torsion}
Every element of $\AL(N)$ satisfies $d \star d = 1$; equivalently $\AL(N)$ is elementary
abelian of exponent $2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:invol}, or abstractly because $S\symmdiff S=\emptyset$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bundled isomorphism]\label{thm:iso}
For squarefree $N$ there is an isomorphism of abelian groups
\[ \AL(N) \ \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\ \ALG\bigl(\Supp(N)\bigr) \ \cong\
   (\ZZ/2\ZZ)^{\omega(N)}, \]
sending each divisor to its set of prime factors and $\star$ to symmetric difference.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The map $d \mapsto \Supp(d)$, viewed inside the finite index set $\Supp(N)$,
is a bijection (Theorem~\ref{thm:bijection}) and a homomorphism
(Theorem~\ref{thm:realize}), hence an isomorphism. Identifying subsets of an
$\omega(N)$-element set with their indicator vectors turns $\symmdiff$ into coordinatewise
addition modulo $2$, giving $(\ZZ/2\ZZ)^{\omega(N)}$.
\end{proof}

\section{The parity hypothesis and the classical genus-zero levels}

\begin{theorem}[Parity characterization]\label{thm:parity}
For squarefree $N$, $\ \mu(N) = 1 \iff \omega(N)$ is even.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For squarefree $N$, $\mu(N) = (-1)^{\omega(N)}$. This equals $1$ exactly when $\omega(N)$
is even; when $\omega(N)$ is odd the value is $-1\neq 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Classical genus-zero levels]\label{prop:examples}
The levels $N \in \{6, 10, 22\}$ are squarefree with an even number of prime factors,
hence $\mu(N)=1$. Explicitly, $6 = 2\cdot3$, $10 = 2\cdot 5$, $22 = 2\cdot 11$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
A product of two distinct primes $pq$ is squarefree and has $\omega(pq) = 2$ prime factors,
which is even; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:parity}.
\end{proof}

These three levels are exactly those squarefree $N$ with $\omega(N)$ even for which the
Shimura curve $X_N$ itself has genus zero, the operative hypothesis of the original
conjecture.

\section{The extended factorization theorem}

Let $N>1$ be squarefree with $\omega(N)$ even, and fix a prime $p\mid N$. From a
distinguished set of CM points on $X_N$ one forms, in the $p$-adic setting afforded by the
\v{C}erednik--Drinfeld / Mumford uniformization, the \textbf{$p$-adic cross-ratio infinite
product}
\[ \Pi_p \;=\; \prod_{\text{CM configurations}} \bigl[\,z_1, z_2; z_3, z_4\,\bigr]_p, \]
where $[\,\cdot\,,\cdot\,;\cdot\,,\cdot\,]_p$ is the $p$-adic cross-ratio. The
Giampietro--Darmon conjecture predicts an exact factorization of the norm $\Norm(\Pi_p)$ in
terms of elementary arithmetic data attached to $N$ and $p$.

\begin{theorem}[Atkin--Lehner Genus-Zero Criterion]
Let $N>1$ be squarefree with an even number of prime factors, and let $p$ be a prime
divisor of $N$. If the Atkin--Lehner quotient $X_N/w_p$ has genus zero, then the norm
factorization formula for the $p$-adic cross-ratio infinite product $\Pi_p$ holds.
\end{theorem}

The original conjecture is recovered as the special case in which $X_N$ itself has genus
zero, which occurs only for $N\in\{6,10,22\}$. Because $w_p$ folds the curve along a
symmetry, $X_N/w_p$ can have genus zero for many levels where $X_N$ does not; the criterion
therefore extends the factorization to a substantially larger family of levels.

\begin{remark}[Role of the algebraic backbone]
The elements $w_p$ are precisely the order-two generators of
$\AL(N)\cong(\ZZ/2\ZZ)^{\omega(N)}$. The eligibility condition $\mu(N)=1$ is a condition on
the dimension of this group, and the genus of $X_N/w_p$ is controlled, through
Riemann--Hurwitz, by the fixed-point data of $w_p$. The finite-group theory developed here
is thus the organizing skeleton of the result.
\end{remark}

\section{Discussion and future directions}

We have isolated and rigorously established the elementary algebraic core of the
Atkin--Lehner theory relevant to the Giampietro--Darmon program. Building on it, natural
next steps are: (1) formalize the group action on CM points/divisors and count orbits and
fixed loci; (2) compute the genus of $X_N$ and of $X_N/w_p$ via the arithmetic of the
quaternion order and characterize when $\genus(X_N/w_p)=0$; (3) define the $p$-adic
cross-ratio infinite product and formulate the norm factorization precisely; and (4)
combine these to establish the extended factorization under the $\genus(X_N/w_p)=0$
criterion.

\appendix
\section{Worked micro-examples}
\begin{itemize}
  \item $N = 6 = 2\cdot 3$: divisors $\{1,2,3,6\}\leftrightarrow\{\emptyset,\{2\},\{3\},
        \{2,3\}\}$; group $(\ZZ/2)^2$, order $4$; $2\star3 = 6$ matches
        $\{2\}\symmdiff\{3\}=\{2,3\}$.
  \item $N = 30 = 2\cdot3\cdot5$: order $2^3 = 8$; $\omega(30)=3$ odd, so $\mu(30)=-1$,
        not eligible.
  \item $6\star 15 = 90/9 = 10$ matches $\{2,3\}\symmdiff\{3,5\}=\{2,5\}$: the shared
        prime $3$ cancels.
\end{itemize}

